INT. GLACIER CREVASSE - ICE CHAMBER - DAY

Blue light filtered through forty feet of ice. The walls drip in slow, patient beads. A shelf of old snow juts out above a bottomless dark. Three climbers lie wedged where the fall stopped them, their breath rising like prayers.

THEO MARSH-ADEYEMI, 40s, broad-shouldered, rope burn weeping through his glove, props himself against the wall. Beside him ISOLDE KARLSEN, 30s, wiry, a headlamp dangling from her wrist, ties a sling from a torn jacket. A few feet farther BO PETRAKIS, 19, the youngest, sits with knees to chest and stares at his boots as if they might be someone else's.

An old handheld radio lies in Isolde's lap, wrapped in a glove for warmth.

ISOLDE
Hold still, Theo.

THEO
I am still.

ISOLDE
You're shaking.

THEO
That's the cold.

ISOLDE
That's your shoulder.

She ties the knot. He hisses. The ice around them creaks softly.

Bo lifts his head.

BO
Is it supposed to do that?

THEO
The ice? It sings. It means it's alive.

BO
I don't like it.

THEO
Nobody does.

Isolde unwraps the radio, thumbs the switch. A hiss of static, thin as a thread.

ISOLDE
Rescue, Rescue, this is Karlsen. Do you copy?

Static. She waits. The ice drips.

ISOLDE (CONT'D)
Rescue, Rescue. Three alive. Repeat, three alive.

A long hiss. Then, faint:

COORDINATOR NAKAGAWA (V.O.)
Karlsen, Nakagawa. Read you weak. Say position.

Isolde closes her eyes, one second, as though she has been holding her breath since morning.

ISOLDE
Crevasse, east of the col. Approximately forty feet down. One injured, shoulder.

NAKAGAWA (V.O.)
Copy. Helicopter is grounded. Weather. Ground team is four hours out.

Theo says nothing. Bo's boots go suddenly very interesting.

ISOLDE
Four hours.

NAKAGAWA (V.O.)
Conserve heat. Conserve battery. Do not sleep.

ISOLDE
Understood.

NAKAGAWA (V.O.)
You'll be fine, Isolde. I mean that.

She smiles, the kind of smile that has no use for the words.

ISOLDE
We'll be here.

She turns the radio off. Sets it back in the glove. Slides over to Bo, shoulder to shoulder.

BO
Four hours.

ISOLDE
That's nothing.

BO
It feels like a year.

ISOLDE
Then tell me a story.

BO
I don't have one.

THEO
Everyone has one.

BO
Mine's boring.

THEO
Good. Boring is what we want.

Bo almost laughs. The sound dies in his throat and becomes a small cough.

BO
When I was eight, my dog got lost in a snowstorm.

ISOLDE
Did you find her?

BO
He came home on his own, three days later. Covered in ice.

THEO
Smart dog.

BO
Stubborn.

The ice sings again, softer. Theo leans his head back.

Overhead, a thread of light from the crevasse mouth slides across the wall, slow as a minute hand, and begins to fade.
